{
  "title": "Rate limiters: burst vs abuse, two designs",
  "content": "The default configuration is fine right up until it isn't. code review was never the hard part. Knowing when it was about to fail was. Here's the version that skips the happy-path tutorial.\n\n## The problem with one bucket\n\nMost rate limiter designs collapse two very different things into one number: a user's legitimate burst and an attacker's abuse. A mobile client reconnecting after a subway ride fires 40 requests in two seconds. A scraper fires 40 requests in two seconds. If your limiter is a single fixed window of 100 requests per minute, both get the same treatment — one gets throttled unfairly, the other gets a free pass until the window rolls over. The interesting engineering question is not \"what limit should I set\" but \"what signal distinguishes a burst from an attack, and what do I do with it.\"\n\nThis post compares two common approaches: a **token bucket with a separate concurrency cap** (call it the layered limiter) and a **sliding-window counter with a penalty box** (call it the reputation limiter). They are not mutually exclusive, and the honest answer is that most production systems end up with pieces of both. But the design philosophies are different enough that picking one first changes everything downstream — your Redis schema, your error responses, your observability, and how you explain a 429 to a support ticket.\n\nI think the layered limiter is the better default for most teams, and I'll argue that below. But it has a real weakness around distributed state that the reputation limiter handles more gracefully, and pretending otherwise would make this comparison useless.\n\n## Option A: token bucket + concurrency cap\n\nThe token bucket is the most widely deployed rate limiting algorithm, and for good reason: it has two parameters that map cleanly onto two different concepts. The **refill rate** (tokens per second) governs sustained throughput. The **bucket size** (maximum tokens) governs how much burst you tolerate. A bucket of 100 tokens refilling at 10/s means a client can burst to 100, then is held to 10/s afterward.\n\nThat already separates burst from sustained abuse. But it does not separate burst from *parallelism*, which is where the second layer comes in. A concurrency cap limits how many requests a single client can have in flight simultaneously. This matters because a burst of 40 requests fired in parallel is a different signal than 40 requests fired over two seconds. The first can exhaust your database connection pool; the second usually cannot.\n\nA minimal implementation using a Redis-backed token bucket looks like this. The Lua script runs atomically on the server, which avoids the read-modify-write race that plagues naive implementations:\n\n```lua\n-- token_bucket.lua\n-- KEYS[1] = bucket key\n-- ARGV[1] = capacity, ARGV[2] = refill_per_sec, ARGV[3] = now_ms, ARGV[4] = cost\nlocal capacity = tonumber(ARGV[1])\nlocal refill = tonumber(ARGV[2])\nlocal now = tonumber(ARGV[3])\nlocal cost = tonumber(ARGV[4])\n\nlocal data = redis.call('HMGET', KEYS[1], 'tokens', 'ts')\nlocal tokens = tonumber(data[1])\nlocal ts = tonumber(data[2])\n\nif tokens == nil then\n  tokens = capacity\n  ts = now\nend\n\nlocal delta = math.max(0, now - ts) / 1000.0\ntokens = math.min(capacity, tokens + delta * refill)\n\nlocal allowed = 0\nif tokens >= cost then\n  tokens = tokens - cost\n  allowed = 1\nend\n\nredis.call('HMSET', KEYS[1], 'tokens', tokens, 'ts', now)\nredis.call('PEXPIRE', KEYS[1], math.ceil(capacity / refill * 1000) + 1000)\n\nreturn {allowed, math.floor(tokens)}\n```\n\nCalling it from a request handler (Python, using a Redis client that supports `EVALSHA`):\n\n```python\nimport time\nimport redis\n\nr = redis.Redis()\nSCRIPT = r.register_script(open('token_bucket.lua').read())\n\ndef allow(user_id: str, cost: int = 1) -> tuple[bool, int]:\n    now_ms = int(time.time() * 1000)\n    allowed, remaining = SCRIPT(\n        keys=[f'rl:{user_id}'],\n        args=[100, 10, now_ms, cost],\n    )\n    return bool(allowed), int(remaining)\n```\n\nThe concurrency cap is separate and usually lives in-process or in a lightweight shared store. An asyncio semaphore per user works if you have a single process; for multiple processes you need a shared counter with a TTL, which is where the distributed-state weakness shows up.\n\nWhere the layered limiter shines: it is simple to reason about, the parameters have physical meaning, and a well-tuned bucket produces almost no false positives on legitimate mobile traffic. Where it struggles: when a client is legitimately distributed (a CI system with many runners behind one API key), the concurrency cap becomes a blunt instrument, and you end up needing per-key overrides that multiply your config surface.\n\n## Option B: sliding-window counter + penalty box\n\nThe reputation limiter takes a different stance. Instead of trying to model the shape of traffic, it counts requests in a sliding window and escalates consequences for clients that repeatedly hit the limit. The window is precise (no fixed-window boundary burst), and the penalty box is the innovation: a client that trips the limit multiple times in a short period gets progressively longer cooldowns, rather than a simple 429 that resets the moment the window rolls.\n\nA sliding-window counter is typically implemented with two Redis keys — the current window and the previous window — and a weighted sum. The math, assuming a 60-second window and a request arriving 15 seconds into the current window:\n\n- Previous window count: 80\n- Current window count: 20\n- Weight on previous = (60 - 15) / 60 = 0.75\n- Estimated count = 20 + 80 × 0.75 = 80\n\nThat estimate is compared against the limit. The approximation is well-documented and is what most \"sliding window\" implementations actually do; the exact sliding log is more accurate but stores one entry per request, which is expensive at scale.\n\nThe penalty box is where the reputation limiter earns its name. A typical policy:\n\n| Trips in last hour | Cooldown |\n| --- | --- |\n| 1 | 0s (normal 429) |\n| 2 | 60s |\n| 3 | 300s |\n| 4+ | 3600s |\n\nThose numbers are illustrative — the point is the shape, not the specific values. The escalation is stored alongside the client identity, often with a longer TTL than the rate limit key itself.\n\nWhere the reputation limiter shines: it handles distributed clients gracefully (each request is just a count), it produces a clear audit trail for abuse investigations, and the penalty escalation is a genuine deterrent against scrapers that would otherwise just retry after every 429. Where it struggles: the sliding window is an approximation, and the penalty box can misfire on legitimate clients that happen to have a bad deploy — a retry storm from your own mobile app can look exactly like an attack.\n\n## Head-to-head: what the algorithms actually guarantee\n\nThe token bucket's guarantee is stated in the algorithm itself: over any interval of length T, the number of requests allowed is at most `capacity + refill × T`. That is a hard bound, and it holds regardless of how requests are distributed. This is why token buckets are the standard choice when you need to protect a downstream resource with a known capacity.\n\nThe sliding-window counter's guarantee is weaker: it bounds requests per window, but the weighted approximation can allow slightly more than the limit at window boundaries. The error is bounded by the weight on the previous window, so the worst case is roughly `limit × (1 + (W-1)/W)` where W is the window length in seconds — for a 60-second window that is under 2× the nominal limit at the boundary, though in practice the approximation is much tighter. Teams that need an exact bound use a sliding log or a token bucket instead.\n\nWhat neither algorithm does on its own is distinguish a burst from an attack. That distinction comes from the *second* layer in each design: the concurrency cap in Option A, the penalty box in Option B. This is the part most tutorials skip, and it is the part that actually matters.\n\n## Head-to-head: developer experience and operational cost\n\nThe layered limiter is easier to tune because its parameters are physical: \"this endpoint can sustain 50 rps and tolerate a 200-request burst.\" You can derive those from load testing the downstream service. The concurrency cap is harder — you need to know your connection pool size, your typical request latency, and how much headroom you want. But once set, the behavior is predictable.\n\nThe reputation limiter is easier to *deploy* because there is only one counter to maintain per client, and the penalty box is just another key. But it is harder to *reason about* in an incident: when a customer complains that they were blocked for an hour, you need to reconstruct their trip history, which means the penalty state has to be durable and queryable. If it lives only in Redis with a short TTL, you lose the audit trail.\n\nBoth designs share the same distributed-state problem: the limiter state must be consistent across all instances handling a given client. Sticky routing by client ID solves this but breaks when instances scale or restart. A centralized store (Redis, or a purpose-built service) is the usual answer, and it introduces a new failure mode — if the store is unavailable, do you fail open (allow everything) or fail closed (block everything)? Most teams fail open for availability and accept the risk, which is a decision worth writing down explicitly.\n\nThere is a security angle here that is easy to miss. Rate limit keys are usually derived from client identity — an API key, a user ID, an IP address. If those keys are guessable or spoofable, an attacker can either exhaust another user's bucket (denial of service against a specific account) or evade their own limit by rotating keys. IP-based limiting is especially fragile behind shared NATs and corporate proxies, where thousands of legitimate users share one address. API-key-based limiting is better but only if the key itself is treated as a secret — which means the same discipline you apply to database credentials applies here: no keys in client-side code, no keys in public repos, rotation on suspected exposure. The well-documented pattern of secrets committed to public repositories is not hypothetical; it is one of the most common ways rate limit keys leak, and once a key is public, the limiter is protecting the wrong identity.\n\n## A decision framework\n\nUse the layered limiter (token bucket + concurrency cap) when:\n\n- Your clients are mostly interactive — mobile apps, browsers, CLI tools — where bursts are natural and expected.\n- You have a downstream resource with a known capacity that you need to protect (a database, a third-party API with its own limits).\n- You can afford the per-endpoint tuning work and want predictable, provable bounds.\n\nUse the reputation limiter (sliding window + penalty box) when:\n\n- Your clients are mostly machine-to-machine — integrations, batch jobs, partner systems — where sustained rate matters more than burst shape.\n- Abuse is a real, ongoing concern and you want escalation to be automatic and auditable.\n- You are willing to accept approximation at window boundaries in exchange for simpler state.\n\nUse both when: you have a public API with mixed client types, which is most public APIs. The layered limiter handles the interactive clients; the penalty box handles the repeat offenders. The two can share the same Redis instance but should use separate key namespaces so a bug in one does not corrupt the other.\n\n## Recommendation and caveats\n\nI would start with the layered limiter for a new API, because its guarantees are stronger and its parameters are easier to justify in a design review. Add a penalty box later, once you have data on which clients actually abuse the system — building the escalation policy before you have that data means guessing at thresholds, and guessed thresholds are how you end up blocking a legitimate customer during their biggest traffic day.\n\nThe weakness of the layered limiter is real: the concurrency cap is hard to tune correctly, and getting it wrong produces either false positives (throttling users who are not actually a problem) or false negatives (letting a parallel flood through). The mitigation is to make the cap configurable per API key and to start it high, then tighten based on observed p99 in-flight counts. Do not set it from a guess.\n\nThe weakness of the reputation limiter is that the penalty box is a policy decision disguised as a technical one. How long do you punish? Does a trip during a known outage count? Can a customer appeal? These are product questions, and they need product answers, not just code.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n**How do I choose between token bucket and sliding window?**\n\nIf you need a provable upper bound on request rate over any interval, use a token bucket — the bound is `capacity + refill × T` and it holds exactly. If you need a simple counter that is cheap to store and you can tolerate a small approximation at window boundaries, a sliding-window counter is fine. The choice is about which guarantee you actually need, not which algorithm is more modern.\n\n**Why does my rate limiter block legitimate users after a deploy?**\n\nA common cause is a retry storm: when a client deploy goes wrong, the client retries failed requests aggressively, and the retry traffic looks identical to an attack. The fix is not to loosen the limiter but to add a retry budget on the client side (exponential backoff with jitter, capped total retries) and to distinguish retried requests from new ones, for example with a header. Without that, any limiter will eventually misfire on your own traffic.\n\n**Should rate limit state live in Redis or in-process?**\n\nIn-process is faster and has no network failure mode, but it only works if a given client always hits the same instance, which is rarely true behind a load balancer. Redis (or another shared store) makes the state consistent across instances but introduces a dependency: if Redis is down, you must choose fail-open or fail-closed. Most teams fail open and log loudly; the alternative is an outage caused by your own protection mechanism.\n\n**How do I rate limit without breaking shared-IP users?**\n\nDo not rate limit on IP alone for authenticated endpoints. Use the authenticated identity (API key, user ID) as the primary key and treat IP as a secondary signal for unauthenticated traffic. If you must limit by IP, set the limit high enough that a large corporate NAT does not trip it, and monitor the distribution of requests per IP so you can see when a single address is behaving anomalously.\n\n## The one thing to check first\n\nOpen your rate limiter configuration right now and find the single number that governs burst tolerance — the bucket capacity or the window limit. Then ask: is that number derived from a measurement of your downstream capacity, or was it copied from an example? If it was copied, you have a tuning task, not a design task, and it is the highest-leverage 30 minutes you can spend on this today. Write down the number, the reason, and the date next to it in the config file.\n\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** October 2026\n",
